ns from OEM and retail realities.\n\n**Revising Engine B Logic**\n\nI'm now rewriting Engine B's fallback pricing logic. Instead of relying on arbitrary class mappings, I'm implementing parametric scaling laws tied to physical capacity. This will ensure component costs are realistically determined by factors like heat exchanger area or motor power.\n\n**Addressing Regulatory Mismatches**\n\nI am currently examining the report's stated regulatory compliance. I've identified a conflict where the brief targets both UK and EU jurisdictions without a clear harmonization plan. This requires a review of the declared mandatory standards to ensure proper coverage.\n\n**Harmonizing Regulations**\n\nI am now reconciling the report's UK market focus with its reliance on EU directives. I'm cross-referencing each listed directive with its UK equivalent to ensure complete and accurate regulatory compliance for the specified target market.\n\n**Reviewing Charge Limits**\n\nI am now scrutinizing the R290 charge limit, noting the report's vagueness about monobloc versus split configurations. The 650g charge might be acceptable outdoors, but its suitability for integration with indoor cylinders, considering flammability standards, is highly questionable and requires immediate clarification.\n\n**Clarifying Charge Limits**\n\nI'm now addressing critical safety and regulatory concerns regarding the R290 charge limit. The report's stated 650g charge, particularly for potential indoor integration, appears non-compliant with stringent BS EN 378 and IEC 60335-2-40 standards without extensive, unmentioned safety measures. I'm also noting a complete omission of crucial UK MCS standards.\n\n**Updating Compliance Mappings**\n\nI am now updating the compliance gate logic. I'm implementing jurisdiction mapping for UK Statutory Instruments and specific MCS standards to rectify outdated EU-centric database issues and ensure accurate adherence to post-Brexit regulations.\n\n**Identifying Fabricated 